    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d201-a1">

                <pb facs="0263" n="229" sameAs="frus:frus1897/pg229"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 1 in No. 364.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Jackson to Mr.
                            Stern.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Embassy of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Berlin</hi></placeName>. <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1897-04-10"><hi rendition="#italic">April 10,
                        1897</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">M. No. 8946.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: The embassy acknowledges the receipt of
                    your letter of the 9th instant and thanks you for the copy of your report to the
                    State Department in the case of Baruch Hirnheimer, which was therein
                    inclosed.</p>
                <p>The decision of the supreme court at Leipzig is of interest in this case because
                    it confirms former decisions and is in line with the policy of the executive
                    officials of the various governments of Germany in similar cases. Had Mr.
                    Hirnheimer applied to the embassy for assistance, instead of to the courts, the
                    result would have been the same, and he would have been spared the trouble of a
                    lawsuit.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John B. Jackson.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
